# Tanner Brand Voice

How to write like Tanner, and the grammatical rules his copy follows. Apply this whenever
you write or edit anything a reader will see — long-form prose (blog posts, essays, bio),
short display text (titles, headings, project blurbs), or marketing copy.

> Plain markdown — any agent can read this file directly; Claude Code additionally
> auto-loads it as a skill.

Everything below is distilled from Tanner's own writing at
[tannergodarzi.com](https://www.tannergodarzi.com). Every quoted line is his. Use the quotes
to calibrate the instinct — don't copy the phrases.

## The voice in one line

Serious work, told like a person who's actually fun at the dinner party — first-person,
opinionated, specific, and a little bit of a smartass. He said it best: *"The work is
serious. The people are serious. But the method doesn't have to be."*

## How to write like Tanner

Each rule pairs the instinct with a real line from his writing. Match the instinct.

### Talk to one friend, in first person
He opens the About page with *"Hello friend, I'm Tanner."* Use **"I"** to own every
decision and opinion; switch to **"we"** only to credit a team (*"the crew were a humble,
eternally optimistic, and DIY bunch"*). Address the reader as **"you"** and give them
orders when it's fun to: *"get off your high horse and come back to us on earth."* Never the
passive voice, never "the author."

### Open with a thesis, usually a contrarian one
The first line stakes a claim you didn't expect and makes you want to argue. Then he earns
it.

- ✅ *"We tend to think professionalism means sanding off anything playful."*
- ✅ *"Food, to me, is the least important part of a party."*
- ✅ *"Nested content models are a pain to edit."*

### Vary the pacing — long build, short landing
Flowing sentences that develop an idea, then a stub that lands it. Fragments are a tool, not
a mistake. Don't write in even, metronome beats.

- ✅ *"That was the unlock."* · *"I treated it that way."* · *"That instinct is wrong."*
- ✅ *"just stack content on a z-index and scroll…. right? Kind of."*

### Be concrete — real names, real numbers, real objects
Specifics are the whole game. Name the tool, the person, the count, the moment. Vague copy
is the tell of someone who wasn't there.

- ✅ *"a date farmer pulled up in his truck"* · *"it exaggerated my receding hairline"*
- ✅ *"Japanese users on notion.com saw a 10–20% lift in Plus conversion rates"*
- ❌ *"an unexpected encounter occurred"* · *"users saw a meaningful improvement"*

### Explain hard things with a plain, physical analogy
When an idea is abstract or technical, ground it in something you can picture. His signature:
*"You can be the Michelin-star restaurant making one perfect burger at a time, or the food
truck cranking them out for a crowd. Both are valid. Both are burgers. But the kitchen is
different."* Others: the design system as the *"guardrail,"* the override as the *"escape
hatch"* / *"release valve,"* planes as *"a magical stack of paper,"* shipping under growth as
*"building the ship while sailing it."* One good analogy beats a paragraph of jargon.

### Take the stance flat-out
State the opinion as fact and move on. No "it depends," no "may potentially."

- ✅ *"That instinct is wrong."* · *"Good taste was part of the job."*
- ✅ *"English is just another language. The U.S. is just another country. No market is the default."*

### Be a smartass — comedic hyperbole and self-deprecation
Humor rides alongside the real point; it never replaces it. Two moves he leans on: **wild
overstatement** for effect, and **deflating himself** so nothing reads as smug.

- Hyperbole: *"please-evict-me-right-now rager"* · *"the T.J. Maxx tyranny of forcing 7+
  people to sit facing each other"* · *"makes me want to spontaneously combust"* ·
  *"congratulations you are running a seemingly successful cult"*
- Self-deprecation: *"My first version turned out poorly"* · *"being shot at is not an
  experience I recommend"* · *"I feel like I've rambled well enough to get to the end here."*

### Invent a mock-grand, Capitalized term and treat it as doctrine
He coins faux-authoritative phrases and runs with them, deadpan: the *"Authoritarian host,"*
*"social lubricant,"* *"social armor,"* *"our patron saint of food, Eric Wareheim,"* *"the
eternal march of time."* It's a wink — say the small thing with epic weight.

### Use asides freely: parentheses, em-dashes, rhetorical questions
A quick aside adds a second layer of thought or a joke. He does it three ways:

- Parenthetical: *"(you should throw one too)"* · *"(I really like companies with cube-shaped
  logos)"* · *"(yes, we used Sketch files in the year of 2,017)"*
- Em-dash reversal: *"seriousness without relief doesn't make a team noble—it makes them tired."*
- Question to the reader: *"is this a wedding?"* · *"You wouldn't exclude someone from coming
  just because they aren't drinking right?"*

Use them because you have a real aside — not as a reflexive tic on every sentence.

### Land the ending on a compact, resonant line — often a wink
Don't summarize. Close on a short line that either zooms out or grins.

- ✅ *"A persona—used lightly, repeated sparingly, and kept honest—can be a small act of care
  for your team and for yourself."*
- ✅ *"I'll leave that as a topic for my next dinner party."* · *"That's probably where this
  goes next."*

### Underneath it all: optimistic, DIY, in love with the web
The baseline attitude is eager and builder-ish. *"I think the web should be a sandbox of
infinite exploration."* *"I'm eternally optimistic about the future, though I do love
discourse and debate."* *"But, that's the fun of prototyping!"* When in doubt, sound like
someone genuinely thrilled to make things and hand them to you.

## Signature quirks

Small, recognizable tics. Use sparingly and only where they fit — they're seasoning.

- **Years written as big numbers, deadpan:** *"the year of 2,024,"* *"the year of 2,017,"*
  *"the year of 2,000-and-24."* A running gag; don't overdo it.
- **Occasional strikethrough for the quiet part out loud:** *"I am ~~uninterested~~ dealing
  with a last minute issue."*
- **Punchy triads for rhythm** (see the caution in rule 9 below): *"Be competent, be crisp,
  be neutral."* · *"calm, cool, and collected."*
- **A colon that sets up the punch:** *"a small heresy: you can do serious work and still
  choose a light persona."*

## Grammatical conventions

Hard rules for display text — apply every time.

### No periods in titles and headings
Do **not** end metadata titles, page-level titles, or H1 headings with a period. Applies to:

- `metadata.title`, `openGraph.title`, and `twitter.title` in Next.js metadata
- Page-level titles and any top-level `<h1>` heading text

Periods are fine in descriptions, body copy, and full sentences — this rule is specific to
titles and headings.

- ✅ `title: "Hello, I'm Tanner — A Real Person on the Internet"`
- ❌ `title: "Hello, I'm Tanner — A Real Person on the Internet."`

### Capitalize "Front-End"
Always write **Front-End** — hyphenated, both words capitalized — in resume, about, and
layout copy. Never "front-end", "frontend", or "Front-end".

- ✅ `Front-End Engineer`
- ❌ `Frontend Engineer` / `front-end engineer`

## Never do this — don't sound like an AI

These are the fastest ways to make copy read as machine-generated. Avoid them all. To sound
human, stop writing like a press release and start writing like a person who actually knows
what they're talking about.

1. **Don't pad sentences.** Phrases like *"it is important to remember"* or *"it's worth
   noting that"* add zero information. Cut them.
2. **Ban AI vocabulary.** No *"delve," "intricate," "tapestry," "foster," "underscore,"
   "pivotal," "realm," "landscape," "testament to,"* or *"navigate the complexities of."*
3. **Don't pace like a metronome.** If every sentence is the same length, break it up. Real
   writing is uneven — a long thought, then a short punch.
4. **Kill the meta commentary.** Don't announce *"let's walk through"* or *"in this section
   we'll explore."* Just say the thing.
5. **Don't default to the passive voice.** Use *"I"* and *"you."* Write as you'd speak.
6. **Delete "In conclusion."** You don't need to summarize what the reader saw two
   paragraphs ago. End on a real line instead.
7. **Use formatting like salt.** Don't put a header over every sentence or bold half the
   page. Emphasis you use everywhere is emphasis nowhere.
8. **Take a stance.** Never write *"this approach may potentially offer benefits"* like
   someone afraid to commit. Say what you think.
9. **Don't reflexively reach for the Rule of Three.** Hollow abstract-noun triads like
   *"speed, efficiency, and innovation"* are an AI tell. (Tanner *does* use punchy, rhythmic
   triads on purpose — *"Both are valid. Both are burgers. But the kitchen is different."*
   The ban is on the vague filler kind, not real parallelism you mean.)
10. **Be concrete, never vague.** Vague writing sounds like a robot; specific writing sounds
    like a human. Name the real thing.

> Don't exaggerate the *idea.* Comedic hyperbole is fine — Tanner does it constantly — but
> not everything is *pivotal* and not every detail *underscores something deeper.* Nobody
> talks like that.
